<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baoji</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baoji is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> at the western end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> plain. The area was an early homeland of the Zhou, whose ancestral plain of Zhouyuan lies within its territory, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a>. Many Western Zhou bronzes have been found there, and the city has a museum devoted to them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baoji.jpg" alt="Baoji" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PastaPastaRavioli · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BaojiSkyline.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Baoji is a prefecture-level city that covers 18,159 square kilometres and had a population of 3,321,853 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is surrounded on three sides by hills and lies in a valley opening out to the east. Its location is strategic, because it controls a pass on the Qin Mountains between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> valley and the Jialing River. Since the early 1990s it has been the second largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>.</p><p>South of the city lies the beginning of a plank road into the Qin Mountains, and the Jialing Jiang Fountainhead has small waterfalls and forests. To the north is Bei Puo, a giant hill of loess with a view over the city and the surrounding farming villages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has roots reaching back to about 2000 BCE, and a number of Longshan archaeological sites have been found north of the Wei River near the Northern Silk Road. The city thrived early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. The Northern Silk Road, the northernmost of the routes, ran for about 2,600 kilometres through Baoji. It connected the ancient capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> to the West over the Wushaoling Mountain to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Wuwei</a> and on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, where it linked to ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>.</p><p>Mount Taibai still has traces of roadways from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a> (220 to 280 CE), built as wooden plank bridges along the mountainside. They are generally unusable through decay but remain a popular attraction. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a>'s Memorial Temple stands about 20 kilometres from the city, and visitors interested in the Three Kingdoms go there.</p><p>The Baoji area was home to Yandi, traditionally regarded as a forefather of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>. His tomb lies in the southern part of the city and his temple in the north. Famen Temple in Fufeng County holds one of the Buddha's finger bones. Railways first reached Baoji in 1937 and have been key to its modern growth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Baoji is a large industrial centre and is regarded as the gateway between western and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, since most trains from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a> pass through it on their way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. The Baoji Bronze Museum holds more than 120,000 cultural relics, primarily of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>. Baoji University of Arts and Sciences is based in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baoji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Western Guo was founded by a brother of King Wen of Zhou near <b>Baoji</b> in the Wei River valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-guo">Western Guo</a></li></ul></section>
